> Customizing Mango - Monitors

Monitor configuration is in ~/.config/mango/monitors.conf

### monitorrule Format

monitorrule=selector,parameters

Selectors (comma-separated, regex match):
name: matches output name (e.g. name:^DP-1$)
make: matches manufacturer
model: matches monitor model
serial: matches serial number

Parameters (comma-separated):
width: horizontal resolution
height: vertical resolution
refresh: refresh rate in Hz
x: horizontal position in layout
y: vertical position in layout
scale: fractional scaling (e.g. 1.0, 1.5, 2.0)
vrr: variable refresh rate (0=off, 1=on)
rr: custom refresh rate
custom: additional custom parameters

### Default SNAPS-OS Configuration

monitorrule=name:^HDMI-A-1$,width:1920,height:1080,refresh:60,x:1920,y:0
monitorrule=name:^DP-1$,width:1920,height:1080,refresh:60,x:0,y:0
monitorrule=name:^DP-2$,width:1920,height:1080,refresh:60,x:3840,y:0

this configures 3 monitors side by side at 1920x1080, 60Hz.

### Cross-Monitor Settings

focus_cross_monitor=1: allow focus to move between monitors.
exchange_cross_monitor=1: allow windows to be moved between monitors.
syncobj_enable=1: enable sync objects for better rendering.

### Tearing

allow_tearing: set to 1 to allow screen tearing (reduces latency).
not set in default config (commented or omitted).

### toggle_monitor

can be used to toggle a specific monitor on/off in scripts.

### Adaptive Sync (VRR)

set vrr=1 in a monitorrule to enable adaptive sync.
also configurable per-monitor in DMS settings (super+S > displays).

### Using DMS Displays Settings

the graphical display settings in DMS (super+S > displays) will modify
monitors.conf for you. manual editing is mainly for advanced setups
or monitor rules that the GUI doesn't expose.
